The mapping-constructor form panics in the same way, as does a plain `<readonly>` cast:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    var m = <readonly & map<int>>{x: 2};
    io:println(m);
}
```
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    readonly r = <readonly>[1, 2];
    io:println(r);
}
```
Both fail at runtime with `error: bad type cast` at the cast line. Expected output: `{"x":2}` and `[1,2]`.

Spec §6.20: "The contextually expected type for expression is the intersection of the contextually expected type of the type-cast-expr and the type described by the type-descriptor". It also says "a type-cast-expr of `<readonly>` can be used both to cause constructors within the expression to construct values with the read-only bit on and to verify..." §6.7.2 adds: "If the applicable contextually expected type is a subtype of readonly, then the mapping will be constructed with its read-only bit on." Tested on `main` @ 222c3007635f.

