Another instance where `T = readonly`. The cast's static type comes out as the whole `readonly` type rather than its intersection with the operand's type, so a valid program is rejected:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    anydata b = <readonly>[1, 2];
    io:println(b);
}
```
Actual:
```
error[SEMANTIC_ERROR]: incompatible type: expected anydata, got nil|boolean|int|float|decimal|string|error|typedesc|handle|function|regexp|readonly|readonly|table|xml<...>|readonly
```
Expected: compiles and prints `[1,2]`. Per §6.20 the operand's contextually expected type is `anydata & readonly`, so the list constructor's static type is a read-only list of anydata. The cast's static type is "the intersection of T and TE'", which is a subtype of `anydata`. (Once this is fixed, it will still hit the runtime `bad type cast` from #1753 / #1557.) Tested on `main` @ 222c3007635f.

