The same missing `IdentifierEscape` decoding also affects resource path segment names, in both client resource access and service attach points.

1. The raw source spelling reaches a path-parameter resource:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

client class C {
    resource function get [string s]() returns string {
        return s;
    }
}

public function main() {
    C c = new;
    string b = c->/foo\-bar;
    io:println(b);
    string d = c->/foo\u{2D}bar;
    io:println(d);
}
```
Actual: `foo\-bar`, then `foo\u{2D}bar`. Expected: `foo-bar`, then `foo-bar`.

2. Two different escape spellings of the same name do not match:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

client class C {
    resource function get foo\-bar() returns string {
        return "foo-bar";
    }
}

public function main() {
    C c = new;
    string d = c->/foo\u{2D}bar;
    io:println(d);
}
```
Actual: `error[SEMANTIC_ERROR]: no matching resource method 'get'`. Expected: compiles and prints `foo-bar`.

3. The service attach point keeps the backslash. With `service /foo\-bar on new L() {}`, where `L.attach` prints its `attachPoint`, the output is `["foo\\-bar"]` with `attachPoint[0].length()` = 8. Expected: `["foo-bar"]` with length 7.

Spec §2: "The IdentifierEscape syntax allows an arbitrary non-empty string to be treated as an identifier". `resource-path-segment-name := identifier`, and §8.3.2 says the attach point is "an array of strings, with one string for each resource-path-segment-name". (Quoted `'foo` vs `foo` in resource paths is tracked in #1116.) Tested on `main` @ 222c3007635f.

